data: {"type":"response.output_text.done","content_index":0,"item_id":"msg_020907230619f317006aa7489dfa4487d2b0c71b61a647b8b7","logprobs":[],"output_index":1,"sequence_number":108,"text":"If by \"purpose\" you mean an intrinsic goal, ants—like other organisms—don't possess conscious purposes; their behavior is shaped by evolution to survive and reproduce. Functionally, ants act as ecosystem engineers: they aerate and mix soil, decompose organic matter, disperse seeds, farm fungi and hemipterans, and control other insect populations. At the colony level their apparent \"purpose\" is the maintenance and propagation of the colony's genes, a process that in turn supports broader ecological networks."}

